Title:
SilentMoonwalk: Implementing a dynamic Call Stack Spoofer

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This post presents a PoC “dynamic call stack spoofer” (SilentMoonwalk) designed to evade userland call-stack-based telemetry used by EDR and anti-cheat engines. It addresses detections that correlate suspicious behavior (e.g., unbacked memory execution, indirect syscalls, LSASS access) with anomalous or missing call stack frames. The technique goes beyond simple return-address spoofing by dynamically crafting unwindable fake frames and desynchronizing real control flow from what the Windows unwinder reconstructs. It leverages Windows x64 exception unwinding metadata (.pdata / RUNTIME_FUNCTION + UNWIND_CODE) to compute frame sizes and build believable stacks, while also hiding the true originating module/RIP during execution (not only during sleep). Core mechanics include selecting frames with UWOP_SET_FPREG and UWOP_PUSH_NONVOL, using a JOP gadget (JMP [RBX]) to redirect execution, and optionally using a stack-pivot (ADD RSP, X) to conceal the gadget chain. It’s primarily useful for red team operators and malware developers building stealthy loaders/implants, and for blue teams to understand and detect advanced stack manipulation tradecraft.

Technical Focus:
- Windows x64 calling convention, stack frames, non-volatile register preservation
- x64 exception unwinding: .pdata, RUNTIME_FUNCTION, UNWIND_INFO, UNWIND_CODE parsing
- Dynamic stack frame size calculation and unwindable fake frame construction
- Call stack spoofing / return address spoofing / thread stack obfuscation
- Control-flow desynchronization via JOP/ROP gadgets (e.g., JMP [RBX], ADD RSP, X)
- EDR/anti-cheat call-stack-based telemetry and indirect syscall detection implications

Use Cases:
- Building loaders/implants that evade call-stack-based detections during sensitive API usage
- Concealing origin modules for suspicious actions (e.g., handle opens, memory ops, thread creation)
- Researching and validating defensive detections for stack tampering/unwind anomalies
- Enhancing sleep/execute evasion by maintaining plausible stacks throughout runtime

Keywords:
SilentMoonwalk, call stack spoofing, stack spoofing, return address spoofing, Windows x64, unwinding, .pdata, RUNTIME_FUNCTION, UNWIND_INFO, UNWIND_CODE, UWOP_SET_FPREG, UWOP_PUSH_NONVOL, RSP, RBP, non-volatile registers, JOP, ROP, JMP [RBX], stack pivot, ADD RSP, EDR telemetry, anti-cheat, indirect syscalls, BaseThreadInitThunk